  "research": {
    "area-1": {
      "text": "Queen Street East, particularly in the Leslieville and Beaches areas, is noted for its array of shops, cafes, and restaurants, with a mix of established businesses and newer, trendy spots.\n\nGerrard Street East, while historically known as \"Little India,\" has also seen revitalization with new cafes and shops emerging, especially west of Jones Avenue.\n\nRecent changes in Toronto's planning policy allow for new small shops and cafes in some residential neighborhoods, particularly on corner lots on community streets, or next to schools, parks, or commercial sites.\n\nHowever, there has been debate and some scaling back of proposals, with concerns about noise, traffic, and disruption from residents.\n\nThe proposal for allowing retail stores with ancillary eating establishments limits dining space, and full sit-down cafes or patios might require additional public approval processes.\n\nNearby concepts and menu checks\n\nOn Queen Street East, \"Limon Beaches\" offers Middle Eastern dishes.\n\nOn Gerrard Street East, \"Lazy Daisy's Cafe\" has been noted for its presence.\n\n\"Bodega Henriette\" is also in the area.\n\n\"Tea N Bannock\" on Gerrard Street East is a cafe that also sells artisan food gifts and serves Indigenous dishes.\n\n\"Grinder\" on Gerrard Street East is described as an indie coffee shop, attracting commuters, then parents with strollers later in the day.\n\nOther cafes mentioned in the broader Toronto area that offer specialty coffee include \"Subtext Coffee Roasters,\" \"The Library Specialty Coffee,\" and \"Pilot Coffee Roasters\" (which began in Leslieville).\n\nAccess and local context\n\nThe area is served by public transit, with Gerrard Street East having streetcar access.\n\nThe general area, including Leslieville and The Beaches, is considered to be undergoing transition with new cafes and shops.\n\nWhat to check on a visit\n\nA visit should focus on understanding the specific zoning regulations for the chosen property regarding a specialty coffee cafe, particularly concerning the allowance for dine-in seating and any potential for a patio, given the recent debates and amendments to city bylaws.\n\nIt would be important to determine if the property is on a \"major street\" or falls under the \"neighbourhood interior\" designation, as this impacts permissible business types and sizes.\n\nCheck for available commercial spaces and their suitability in terms of size and layout for a cafe concept, considering potential restrictions on kitchen facilities.",
